``` Introduction

Capitalization tells Ore what something is, instead of a keyword. UPPERCASE is a constant (`ISLAND`), Capitalized is a type (`Survivor`), lowercase is a variable or function (`name`, `push_button`). A type is declared with `Identifier { body }` -- no `class` keyword needed, the capital letter already said it.
```
Survivor {
	name,
	number,

	new { name, number;
		./name   = name
		./number = number
	}

	to_s {;
		"`name` (`number`)"
	}
}

jack := Survivor('Jack Shephard', 23)
@puts "jack: `jack.to_s()`"   # jack: Jack Shephard (23)


``` Static declarations: the hatch

The `../` prefix declares (and accesses) a STATIC member -- shared across every instance of the type, instead of one copy per instance. `../presses` is one single counter, not one per Hatch.
```
Hatch {
	../presses := 0

	../push_button {;
		presses += 1
	}

	enter {;
		../push_button()
	}
}

Hatch().enter()
Hatch().enter()
Hatch().enter()
@puts "Hatch.presses: `Hatch.presses`"   # Hatch.presses: 3 -- every Hatch() shares the same counter


``` Scope operators

Three operators reach into a specific scope explicitly, rather than searching outward through everything in reach: `./` is the current instance only, `../` is the current type (static members) only, `~/` is the global scope only. All three skip the normal "search everything in reach" fallback a bare identifier gets -- each looks in exactly one place, so a name collision elsewhere can't quietly shadow the one you meant.
```
ISLAND := 'The Island'

Dharma_Station {
	../station_count := 0
	station_name,

	new { station_name;
		./station_name = station_name
		../station_count += 1
	}

	describe {;
		"`./station_name` on `~/ISLAND` (station #`../station_count`)"
	}
}

swan   := Dharma_Station('The Swan')
orchid := Dharma_Station('The Orchid')
@puts "swan.describe(): `swan.describe()`"       # swan.describe(): The Swan on The Island (station #2) -- ../station_count is shared, not per-instance
@puts "orchid.describe(): `orchid.describe()`"   # orchid.describe(): The Orchid on The Island (station #2), same shared counter


``` Privacy convention

An identifier starting with `_` is private by convention -- reachable from inside the type's own methods, but calling it from outside raises `Cannot_Call_Private_Instance_Member`.
```
Smoke_Monster {
	_true_agenda,
	name,

	new { name, agenda;
		./name          = name
		./_true_agenda  = agenda
	}

	confess { claim;
		claim == ./_true_agenda
	}
}

monster := Smoke_Monster('Smoke Monster', 'control the Island')
@puts "confess('control the Island'): `monster.confess('control the Island')`"   # confess('control the Island'): true
@puts "confess('help the survivors'): `monster.confess('help the survivors')`"   # confess('help the survivors'): false
# monster._true_agenda would raise Cannot_Call_Private_Instance_Member -- outsiders don't get to ask directly


``` Composition: | union

Ore has no inheritance -- types compose instead, with four operators borrowed from set theory. `|` (union) merges every declaration from the right side into the left, with the LEFT side winning any name conflicts. Multiple operators chain freely:
```
Castaway {
	survived_815 := true
}

Other {
	survivor := false
}

Jack | Castaway {
	name := 'Jack'
}

Richard | Other {
	name := 'Richard'
}

Steve | Castaway | Other {
	name := 'Steve'
}

s := Steve()
@puts "s.survived_815: `s.survived_815`"   # s.survived_815: true, from Castaway
@puts "s.survivor: `s.survivor`"           # s.survivor: false, from Other


``` Composition: & intersection, ~ difference, ^ symmetric difference

The other three operators only make sense once two sides might disagree. `Castaway` and `Other` both declare a `faction` method (with different bodies) here, alongside their own unique members, to show how each operator treats a genuine name collision differently:

  &  keeps only what BOTH sides declare (here: just `faction` -- the shared name)
  ~  removes the right side's declarations from the left (drops `faction`, keeps `survived_815`)
  ^  keeps only what's unique to each side (drops the shared `faction`, keeps everything else)
```
Castaway {
	survived_815 := true

	faction {;
		'Survivors'
	}
}

Other {
	survivor := false

	faction {;
		'Others'
	}
}

both := (Castaway & Other)()
@puts "both.faction(): `both.faction()`"   # both.faction(): Survivors -- the only shared name, left side's version wins

just_theirs := (Castaway ~ Other)()
@puts "just_theirs.survived_815: `just_theirs.survived_815`"   # just_theirs.survived_815: true -- Castaway's own, un-shared member survives

unique := (Castaway ^ Other)()
@puts "unique.survived_815: `unique.survived_815`"   # unique.survived_815: true -- unique to Castaway
@puts "unique.survivor: `unique.survivor`"   # unique.survivor: false -- unique to Other


``` A composition chain needs no name at all

Any of the four operators can stand alone with no `{ }` body and no name -- `(Castaway & Other)()` above already did exactly this. It builds an anonymous type on the spot by applying the chain, exactly as if you'd written `X | Castaway | Other { }` for some unnamed `X` -- usable immediately, assignable, comparable, instantiable, without ever needing a name of its own.
```
Survivors_And_Others := Castaway & Other   # a Type value, just like any named one -- not an instance yet
survivor             := Survivors_And_Others()
@puts "survivor.faction(): `survivor.faction()`"   # survivor.faction(): Survivors


``` Type comparison operators

Five operators compare the composed-type SET two types or instances carry -- every type a value's own type composes, via any of the four operators above, plus its own name. All five share the `=X=` shape:

  ===  exact type-set equality
  =!=  negation of ===
  =>=  is left a superset of right (left composes at least everything right does)
  =<=  mirror of =>= with operands reversed
  =/=  disjoint -- the two share no composed type at all

Only `=>=` carries genuinely new information; the rest are readability sugar built on it, the same reason most languages ship both `<=`/`>=` alongside `==`/`!=`.
```
@puts "Jack === Jack: `Jack === Jack`"             # Jack === Jack: true  -- identical composed-type sets
@puts "Jack === Richard: `Jack === Richard`"       # Jack === Richard: false -- Jack composes Castaway, Richard composes Other
@puts "Jack =!= Richard: `Jack =!= Richard`"       # Jack =!= Richard: true
@puts "Steve =>= Castaway: `Steve =>= Castaway`"   # Steve =>= Castaway: true  -- Steve composes with at least everything Castaway does
@puts "Castaway =>= Steve: `Castaway =>= Steve`"   # Castaway =>= Steve: false -- Castaway doesn't compose Steve's extra (Other) side
@puts "Castaway =<= Steve: `Castaway =<= Steve`"   # Castaway =<= Steve: true  -- mirror of the line above
@puts "Jack =/= Richard: `Jack =/= Richard`"       # Jack =/= Richard: true  -- share nothing (Castaway vs Other, no overlap)
@puts "Castaway =/= Other: `Castaway =/= Other`"   # Castaway =/= Other: true  -- the two traits themselves share nothing either
